buckle up game of thrones fans this  bizarre break from canon has literally  changed the course of westerosi history  keep watching to find out how  considering the abysmal fan response  that the final season of game of thrones  received it's not surprising that many  viewers were skeptical of house of the  dragon the latest show to come from  george r martin's a song of ice and fire  indeed the series was already dealing  with some ugly backlash before its  premiere as the casting of lord corliss  valarian was met with a slew of racist  messages across social media in addition  to this hateful backlash many other fans  claimed that they refused to watch the  show on principle alone citing the  unsatisfying conclusion to game of  thrones and the different route that  series took to the books narrative  fortunately one major difference between  the two shows is that house of the  dragon is based on a fully completed  story which is chronicled throughout  martin's fire and blood as such you  might assume that house of the dragon  will have a much easier time sticking to  the cannon of a song of fice and fire  which ought to appease some of the more  vocal skeptics of the series  unfortunately for some however it  appears that the show has already broken  from the book series canon in its very  first episode episode one of house of  the dragon features the scene in which a  young rhaenyra targaryen and alison  hightower read a book together deep  within the red keeps godswood i want to  fly with you and dragon back see the  great wonders across the narrow scene  eat only cake  behind them is a white weirwood tree  with a face carved into it which is  traditionally worshiped by followers of  the old gods the inclusion of this  particular weirwood not only breaks the  canon of the book series but of game of  thrones itself in the books the king's  landing god's wood does not have a  weirwood but an old oak wrapped in smoke  berry vines the false weirwood appears  during eddard stark's stay in king's  landing when he and his daughters pray  to the tree and give their thanks for  bran stark's miraculous survival we also  see the red keeps god's wood in the  fourth season of game of thrones episode  two swords when sansa stark visits it  and conspires with cerdanto's hollard in  both appearances there is no mention or  sight of the massive weirwood that  appears in house of the dragon making  its inclusion in the prequel series a  bizarre break from the established canon  of the show and the books although some  fans might chalk this up to the simple  fact that house of the dragon takes  place more than a century before the  events of game of thrones it's worth  remembering that we already know what  happened to the weirwoods in southern  westeros until this point it's been  implied that if king's landing ever had  a weirwood it would have been destroyed  by the andals when they first came to  westeros george r martin's the world of  ice and fire explains that when the  andals first invaded westeros they burnt  down most of the indigenous people's  god's woods in order to make room for  the faith of the seven the destruction  of these godswoods was more concentrated  in the south than it was in the north  though the text does clarify that many  of the more important southern castles  were allowed to keep their god's woods  as a gesture of good faith although some  might take this to mean that the red  keeps weirwood was spared and might have  actually existed during the time of  house of the dragon king's landing as a  whole wasn't even built until the  invasion of aegon the conqueror several  thousand years after the andals invaded  so why would the andals spare this  specific tree in a godswood for a castle  that was not yet built we don't know the  truth is that the inclusion of the  weirwood tree and house of the dragon  amends the history we thought we knew  about king's landing and establishes  that at one point in time there was a  weirwood growing deep within the red  keep that said it will be interesting to  see what becomes of the tree as the  series goes on as its absence in game of  thrones suggests we should get some  answers  eventually
 

